<UserControl x:Class="ChronoMock.App.Views.SessionPhaseView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:app="clr-namespace:ChronoMock.App"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:ChronoMock.App.Views"
             Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}">

  <!--
    THE SECOND OF THE THREE PHASES: a session under way.

    🔴 WHAT THIS SCREEN ANSWERS, and why it is not the setup screen with extra blocks. On the shipped
    panel a running session leaves the whole form standing - target, date, zone, shift, mode, three
    checkboxes - and pushes the two clocks below the fold, with the verdict and the audit below THEM.
    Measured on panel-coverage-warnings: at the window's height the clocks are the last thing visible and
    the answer is not visible at all. So the phases replace each other. Once a session is running, the
    form has done its job and the clocks are the subject.

    🔴 THE ORDER IS THE ORDER THE QUESTIONS ARRIVE IN. What does the application think the time is (the
    clocks, at the top, as big as they get). How fast is it going and can I move it (the controls, under
    them, because that is what this phase is FOR). Did the substitution actually work (the verdict, in the
    footer beside the status, where the setup phase keeps its contract). What exactly was covered (the
    audit, folded, because it is evidence and not the answer).

    🔴 THE RATE IS READ FROM THE CORE, NOT FROM THE BUTTONS. The preset buttons are imperative: pressing
    one sends a command that can fail, so a row of buttons with the current one highlighted would be
    showing an INTENTION as a fact. The line under the clocks states what the core last reported, which is
    the only rate anybody can rely on.

    🔴 WIRED to the session's commands (the product transition wired the phases). Each rate and jump button
    binds Commands.X and the reported rate is read from the core, never from the buttons.

    🔴 IT MERGES THE PARTS LIBRARY LOCALLY, and keeps doing so despite App.xaml now merging Parts
    application-wide: a {StaticResource PartX} is resolved as this control's BAML loads, and that lookup never
    reaches Application.Resources.
  -->

  <UserControl.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>
      <!-- The local Parts merge STAYS despite App.xaml merging Parts application-wide: a {StaticResource
           PartX} is resolved as this control's BAML loads, and that lookup never reaches Application.Resources
           (see SetupPhaseView for the measured revert). The app-wide merge is for the calculator's implicit
           styles, which are applied at tree time. -->
      <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="../Themes/Parts.xaml" />
      </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
      <!--
        One button per rate and per jump, each an instance of one drawing differing by a label - the shape
        that is a list, not nine copies. The shipped panel writes them out one at a time, which is why
        adding a rate there means editing markup.

        Two templates rather than one, because a speed and a jump run DIFFERENT commands with different
        parameters (a multiplier, a delta) - the only difference between them, the drawing is the same. The
        command comes from the view model on the UserControl (the item itself is a static option that knows
        no view model), and the parameter comes from the item.

        🔴 THE GAP COMES AFTER EACH BUTTON, NEVER BEFORE IT (ControlRowGap). A uniform margin pushed the
        first button 4 px into its own column, so a row no longer started where the line under it did -
        measured on the first render of this phase: both rows at x=99, the reported speed and the jump
        note at x=95.
      -->
      <DataTemplate x:Key="SpeedButton">
        <Button Content="{Binding LabelKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                Margin="{StaticResource ControlRowGap}"
                Command="{Binding DataContext.Commands.Speed, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=UserControl}}"
                CommandParameter="{Binding Multiplier}" />
      </DataTemplate>
      <DataTemplate x:Key="JumpButton">
        <Button Content="{Binding LabelKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                Margin="{StaticResource ControlRowGap}"
                Command="{Binding DataContext.Commands.Jump, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=UserControl}}"
                CommandParameter="{Binding Delta}" />
      </DataTemplate>

      <!-- Wrapping, because nine buttons at the window's floor width do not fit on one line and a row
           that cannot wrap pushes its last button off the card instead. -->
      <ItemsPanelTemplate x:Key="ControlRow">
        <WrapPanel />
      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <!-- The clocks and the audit scroll, the action and the answer do not - the same division the setup
       phase makes, for the same reason: at the window's floor of 360 px everything else goes under the
       fold and Stop would have to be hunted for. -->
  <Grid>
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <RowDefinition Height="*" />
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <ScrollViewer x:Name="FormScroll" Grid.Row="0"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
      <StackPanel Margin="{StaticResource ScrollFormInset}">

        <!--
          THE TWO CLOCKS, side by side and equal. This is the one place two columns are right: both halves
          always have something to show, which is exactly what the setup phase's right-hand column did
          not. Equal width because neither is the more important one - the fake clock is the product's
          whole point and the real one is what makes it legible.
        -->
        <!-- 🔴 WHICH APPLICATION THIS IS, and it was missing. Replacing the form with this phase took the
             answer to "what am I looking at" off the screen with it - the shipped panel carried it only
             because the form stayed standing. Somebody who comes back to the window ten minutes later, or
             runs two sessions in an afternoon, has nothing else to tell them which one this is. First
             line, above the clocks, because it is the subject and they are the predicate.

             It says Application rather than Running because the line outlives the run: the same screen
             shows a session that has ended, and "Running:" above "Session ended" contradicted itself. -->
        <WrapPanel Visibility="{Binding HasTarget, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Text="{DynamicResource session.application_label}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     Text="{Binding TargetName}" />
        </WrapPanel>

        <!-- A pair, drawn by PartSideBySide rather than by a margin on each clock. Named, because the layout
             guard measures the two clocks against the card under them. -->
        <HeaderedContentControl Style="{StaticResource PartSideBySide}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}">
          <HeaderedContentControl.Header>
            <ContentControl x:Name="FakeClock" Template="{StaticResource PartClock}" DataContext="{Binding Fake}" />
          </HeaderedContentControl.Header>
          <ContentControl x:Name="RealClock" Template="{StaticResource PartClock}" DataContext="{Binding Real}" />
        </HeaderedContentControl>

        <!--
          THE CONTROLS, never folded. Changing the rate and jumping the clock in flight are what a person
          opened this phase to do, and a section that had to be opened first would be a setting rather
          than a control.
        -->
        <!-- 🔴 GONE ONCE THE SESSION IS OVER, and only then. A finished session kept this card on screen
             disabled, nine buttons nobody could press. While it is stopping the card stays, disabled, so it
             does not vanish under the pointer that just pressed Stop. -->
        <Border x:Name="ControlsCard" Style="{StaticResource PartCard}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapXl}"
                Visibility="{Binding ShowsSessionControls, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}"
                Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True">
          <StackPanel IsEnabled="{Binding IsRunning}">
            <HeaderedContentControl Style="{StaticResource PartFormRow}">
              <HeaderedContentControl.Header>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" Text="{DynamicResource setup.label_mode}" />
              </HeaderedContentControl.Header>
              <WrapPanel>
                <ItemsControl x:Name="SpeedPresets"
                              ItemsSource="{x:Static app:TimeInputs.SessionSpeeds}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource SpeedButton}"
                              ItemsPanel="{StaticResource ControlRow}" />
                <!-- Any rate at all, for the one that is not a preset. Set passes the box's text to the
                     command, which parses "N" or "xN" and surfaces a bad value as an in-flight error rather
                     than a silent no-op (rule 6). -->
                <!-- Wide enough for its own PROMPT rather than for its content. A box sized to hold three
                     digits cannot show the words that say what it is for, and a placeholder clipped to
                     "any…" explains nothing. Measured on the first render of this row. -->
                <!-- The same gap as the buttons before it, so the row follows one rule rather than three. -->
                <TextBox x:Name="CustomSpeedBox" Width="{StaticResource ShiftUnitWidth}"
                         Margin="{StaticResource ControlRowGap}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                         FontFamily="{StaticResource FontFamilyMono}"
                         app:PartState.Placeholder="{DynamicResource session.rate_placeholder}"
                         AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource session.rate_placeholder}" />
                <Button Content="{DynamicResource action.set_speed}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        Margin="{StaticResource ControlRowGap}"
                        ToolTip="{DynamicResource tip.set_speed}"
                        Command="{Binding Commands.SetCustomSpeed}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding Text, ElementName=CustomSpeedBox}" />
              </WrapPanel>
              <!-- 🔴 THE RATE THE CORE REPORTS, in the row's own message slot - directly under the
                   buttons that ask for it, which is where a reader looks after pressing one. It stood on
                   its own line between the clocks and this card on the first render and read as an
                   orphan: a fact about the buttons, floating above them. -->
              <HeaderedContentControl.Tag>
                <WrapPanel x:Name="SpeedFact">
                  <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Text="{DynamicResource setup.contract_speed}" />
                  <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                             Text="{Binding MultiplierText}" />
                </WrapPanel>
              </HeaderedContentControl.Tag>
            </HeaderedContentControl>

            <!-- 🔴 Ordered as a timeline reads, earliest on the left, so the row is a line with a
                 direction rather than four options in an arbitrary order. -->
            <HeaderedContentControl Style="{StaticResource PartFormRow}">
              <HeaderedContentControl.Header>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" Text="{DynamicResource session.label_jump}" />
              </HeaderedContentControl.Header>
              <ItemsControl x:Name="JumpButtons"
                            ItemsSource="{x:Static app:TimeInputs.SessionJumps}"
                            ItemTemplate="{StaticResource JumpButton}"
                            ItemsPanel="{StaticResource ControlRow}" />
              <HeaderedContentControl.Tag>
                <TextBlock x:Name="JumpNote" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}"
                           Text="{DynamicResource session.jump_note}" />
              </HeaderedContentControl.Tag>
            </HeaderedContentControl>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- THE AUDIT, in all three of its states - waiting, never coming, folded with its counts. One
             component, because the result phase shows the same block once the session is over and the
             two must not drift apart: the lists in it carry untouchable rule 4 on both screens. -->
        <views:AuditSectionView />
      </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>

    <!--
      THE ANSWER AND THE ACTION, pinned, exactly where the setup phase keeps its contract and its Start.

      The status is the largest text in it, because "Running" and "Session ended" are the difference
      between watching and reading. The verdict rides beside it as a chip - colour AND a word AND a glyph,
      never colour alone (zasady/13 section 9), and absent until a verdict exists rather than showing a
      placeholder nobody can act on.
    -->
    <Border Grid.Row="1" Style="{StaticResource PartFooter}">
      <StackPanel>
        <WrapPanel>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartHeading}"
                     Text="{Binding StatusKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}" />
          <Border Style="{StaticResource PartBadge}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceSm}"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Visibility="{Binding VerdictKnown, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
              <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}"
                         Text="{Binding VerdictKind, Converter={StaticResource VerdictGlyph}}"
                         Foreground="{Binding VerdictKind, Converter={StaticResource VerdictBrush}}" />
              <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                         Text="{Binding VerdictLabelKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                         Foreground="{Binding VerdictKind, Converter={StaticResource VerdictBrush}}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </WrapPanel>

        <!-- 🔴 THE ERROR COLOUR HERE, unlike the setup phase's refusal line. Nothing has gone wrong on a
             screen somebody has just opened, but a command that failed MID-SESSION is a thing that went
             wrong, and the reader has to be able to tell it from the status above.

             🔴 AT THE SIZE OF PROSE (PartErrorNote). It is a sentence, often past a hundred characters,
             and set as a caption it read as the least important line in the footer while being the one
             thing in it that had gone wrong. Named for the layout guard, which holds it to the note under
             Stop. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="InFlightError" Style="{StaticResource PartErrorNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Text="{Binding InFlightErrorKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}"
                   Visibility="{Binding ShowsInFlightError, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />

        <!-- 🔴 STOP IS NOT THE ACCENT. The accent on this library means "the next step", and while a
             session runs there is no next step - the reader is watching. An accented Stop would be the
             screen asking to be ended. It is an ordinary button, and it says what it does in a word. -->
        <Button x:Name="StopButton" Content="{DynamicResource control.stop}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource tip.stop}"
                Command="{Binding Commands.Stop}"
                Visibility="{Binding ShowsSessionControls, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}" />

        <!-- 🔴 OUT OF THE TOOLTIP. "The app returns to real time - it is not closed" was already written,
             and it was written where somebody has to hover to find it - so the one question a reader
             actually has before pressing a button called Stop ("does this kill the application I am
             testing?") was answered only after they had decided to risk it. This library's own comment on
             the QPC option says a consequence that lives in a tooltip is one most people meet after the
             fact. Same fault, same fix. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="StopNote" Style="{StaticResource PartNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Text="{DynamicResource session.stop_note}"
                   Visibility="{Binding IsRunning, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
